---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "Local-First Telemetry for Agent Workflows - Harness Dreams";
const description =
  "An architecture note on why Harness Dreams keeps sensitive agent telemetry local by default.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="article-page">
      <header class="article-hero">
        <div class="container section-narrow">
          <span class="eyebrow">Blog / Architecture note</span>
          <h1>Local-first telemetry for agent workflows.</h1>
          <p class="lead">
            Agent sessions contain code, secrets, file paths, business context,
            and every correction you made. The default place for that data is
            the machine where it was created.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="container article-grid">
        <aside class="article-aside card">
          <img
            src="/story-overnight-report.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="aside-image"
          />
          <span class="mono">Architecture</span>
          <strong>Useful before cloud.</strong>
          <p>The local engine should produce value before any server exists.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="article-body">
          <p>
            The product starts with deterministic signals: sessions, tool
            results, validation attempts, accepted changes, and repeated
            corrections. Those signals can be scored locally without turning
            private transcripts into a remote dependency or asking an LLM to
            invent a number.
          </p>

          <h2>Local metrics come first</h2>
          <p>
            The Dream Score is strongest when it is computed from observable
            behavior. Re-ask rate, hedges, tool success, cache reuse, context
            pressure, focus blocks, and breaks do not require a cloud model to
            be useful.
          </p>

          <h2>LLMs should write words, not scores</h2>
          <p>
            Some captions and advice can benefit from model phrasing. The
            trigger and the numbers still come from deterministic local logic,
            and a runner-off mode should look intentional, not broken.
          </p>

          <h2>Sharing should be explicit</h2>
          <p>
            The MVP tests the brag loop with exported share cards, not accounts
            or live sharing. A card is a previewed image with selected data, not
            a background upload of raw work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</SiteLayout>
